mand, focusing on proper exit codes, permission error reporting, and validation for invalid mode specifications.\n\nI'm reconsidering my approach here—I realize I've been making assumptions about what the SRD requirements actually specify without having the full content. I should be more honest about that uncertainty while still giving the stitch agent something concrete to work with. My best guess at what each requirement covers, paired with a reference to the SRD as the authoritative source, strikes the right balance between being specific and actionable without overcommitting to details I'm not certain about. The agent will read the actual SRD and adjust if my interpretation is off.\n\nI also need to be careful not to invent design decisions that aren't grounded in the project architecture or standard practices. The SIGPIPE handler comes from the protocol in CLAUDE.md, os.Chmod and filepath.WalkDir are standard Go approaches—those are defensible. But something like `--reference` support is pure speculation, and I should probably leave that out unless I'm more confident it's in the SRD.\n\nI can't actually read requirements.yaml to check weights, so I'll note that I'm assuming default weight 1 for each requirement and flag that assumption in my output. The task asks me to list weights from the file, but without access to it, I'll do my best with what I can infer. Looking at the code_status, chmod hasn't been started yet with no existing files, so task 0 will create main.go from scratch. The only pkg/ dependency I need is pkg/sys for the SIGPIPE handler that all cmd/ utilities require.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":46489,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":46489},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent